  [38;5;153msession.js[39m assigns a [38;5;153mSession[39m global and also does a guarded [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m so Node tests can [38;5;153mrequire[39m it. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m
  adds a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m; [38;5;153mapp.js[39m keeps its name and its globals.
  [3mBuys:[0m the smallest diff to existing files; nothing is renamed. [3mCosts:[0m the [38;5;153mtypeof module !== "undefined"[39m shim is
  exactly the kind of cleverness worth avoiding, the dependency between the two script tags is positional and invisible,
  and every new page must remember to include both in the right order. [3mRules out:[0m clean imports later, without a second
  migration.

  [1mC. Observable session store (pub/sub)[0m
  Either wiring above, plus subscribe/notify so consumers react to identity changes.
  [3mI'd discard this one:[0m your forms are separate page loads, and a fresh page reads the value once at startup.
  Subscriptions only pay off inside a single long-lived page, which you don't have. Cheap to add later if a real-time
  need appears.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m The deciding factor is that "other forms will need it later" makes the [3mimport story[0m the thing
  you'll touch most, and B's answer to that is a positional script-tag ordering that breaks silently — a new page that
  forgets [38;5;153msession.js[39m, or loads it second, gets [38;5;153mundefined[39m at runtime rather than an error. A's cost is a one-line HTML
  edit and a rename; B's cost recurs on every page you add. A also gets you a test runner for free, where B still needs
  one.

  The [38;5;153m.mjs[39m extension is deliberate: it keeps [38;5;153msrc/[39m's CommonJS working untouched, rather than setting [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m and breaking two files unrelated to this task.
